  "hypothesis": "H: Current Executor V3 emits accepted before starting its worker, and on V39 paired-signal hard invalidation, the existing cancel_invalidated_cover helper sends cancel to that executor, whose worker stops and releases the actual active Xvfb key through InputOwner V4/V12; the planner answer completed afterward remains ineligible.",
  "test": "T: One hard-health arm: retained source sequence2 health/ammo=97/48 and current sequence3 synthetic health=80/ammo=48, shared sample epoch and binding rebound to focused Xvfb target. Current-main Executor V3 runs a bounded hold program through a thin Xvfb backend that delegates all key effects/releases to current-main InputOwner. The actual accepted event and X KeyPress are timestamped; during the pending PersistentPlannerAdapter turn, the current V39 monitor sees the hard invalidation and current cancel helper speaks through an in-process protocol bridge to Executor.cancel and waits for its actual terminal/release receipt. One candidate invocation; no game/model/physical input.",
  "decision": "D: PASS_METHOD_SCOPED only if Executor accepted_ns precedes observed X KeyPress; key remains down when observation arrives; paired health guard invalidates; helper matches cancel; Executor terminal is cancelled with verified empty release; independent Xvfb KeyRelease/keymap confirms up before planner wait returns; planner marks completed answer ineligible/cancelled; final admission rejects policy invalidation; worker and owner terminate; Xvfb exits 0. Any mismatch is STOP.",
  "confounds": "C: Health=80 is synthetic. Executor V3 class and cancellation/release lifecycle are current-main, but its backend is a test adapter to the real Xvfb InputOwner rather than the production controller subprocess. Planner answer is deterministic mock output. No game or physical device.",
  "unknowns": "U: No semantic visual threat, task success/feedback, live MAP01 allocation, subprocess/pipe failure, hard-kill recovery, scheduling stress, network isolation, or hard CPU/memory cap. This tests a guard event, not perception.",
